The Advanced Practitioner (ANP) is an experienced, and appropriately qualified Registered Nurse who manages
the complete clinical care for their patient, not solely any specific condition. The trainee ANP will work in
collaboration with senior Healthcare practitioners, ANPs and GPs to develop advanced clinical skills in practice
alongside completion of accredited PG cert in Advanced practice to deliver care.
The ANP/trainee ANP will predominantly focus on Urgent and Unscheduled Care referrals in the management of
adults and children within the acute and community setting including nursing and care home residents; residents in
respite beds; and the complex treatment and management of acutely unwell, palliative or frail patients. The
ANP/trainee ANP will be the first response for the provision of Urgent and unscheduled care, NHS 24 appointments
and home visits in the community. The Trainee ANP may be required to undertake initial assessment of paediatric
referrals for NHS 24, onward referral to a senior clinician and collaborate on management plan.
The ANP/trainee ANP will demonstrate advanced practice skills to assess, diagnose, treat (including non-medial
prescribing) and monitor patients within the community setting. Working as part of the Integrated Front Door team,
ANPs will be required to work in, or across all clinical settings, including custody care, The Emergency Department,
AAU (site dependent) and Hospital @Home dependent on daily clinical demand and available skill mix.
The ANP/trainee ANP will provide clinical leadership, to promote high professional standards, facilitate learning,
provide clinical supervision and mentorship and act as a nurse advisor to members of the Community Nursing Team
and the wider Health and Social Care Partnership.
The ANP will have a role in participating in national research, audit and contribute to the development of new
pathways of care as necessary to meet the needs of patients, including frailty and OPAT.   The post holder has a shared responsibility for the service therefore must work in collaboration with the
District Nurses, GPs, CPNs, Nursing and Care Home staff, Emergency Department, ENPS, NHS24, SAS,
AAU and H@H, Police Scotland and other members of the multidisciplinary team.
Clinical Practice
• Triage patients who are referred, ensuring patients are seen according to clinical urgency and need, and
where appropriate referring the patient to the most appropriate team member for ongoing management and
review.
• Provide an advanced role in the assessment, treatment (including non-medical prescribing),
monitoring and support for patients who have complex physical, psychological and social problems
in a range of clinical settings including Community Hospitals; NHS 24 appointments; Domiciliary
Settings, custody care and including patients own homes and Nursing/Care home settings.
• Provide complete, individualised clinical care to patients within your knowledge and expertise, referring to
other Team members as appropriate.
• Manage, organise, coordinate own urgent care caseload and support the caseload management of other
members of the Community Nursing Teams. • Facilitate and coordinate admission and discharge in discussion with, and agreement from, the receiving
clinician to NHS Western Isles inpatient areas.
• Refer to other health and social care services and agencies.
• Liaise with other members of the MDT and partake in MDT meetings to support patients care planning.
• Provide education to patients and colleagues to support self-management and provide Anticipatory Care
Plans as appropriate.
• Participate in weekly MDTs with Care Homes, Macmillan Services and GP Practices
Leadership
• Provide professional leadership through sharing clinical expertise, provide clinical supervision, mentorship
and advice to nursing colleagues.
• Facilitate the delivery of excellent patient centred care through the promotion of high professional standards
and evidence- based practice.
• In the absence of team lead, ensure safe rota compliance Facilitation of Learning
• Recognise and facilitate learning needs within the Community Nursing Team and the wider Health and Social
Care Partnership.
• Provide a learning environment for clinical staff and students from a variety of disciplines, studying at various
levels.










